ABSTRACT

Future permanent human settlements on the Moon and Mars will operate under physical and environmental conditions of extreme lethality, in which no immediate rescue is possible and communication latency with Earth may reach up to 24 minutes each way. Existing passive safety paradigms — inherited from terrestrial and low-Earth-orbit architectures — are fundamentally inadequate for the multi-hazard, multi-module, multi-occupant demands of interplanetary colonies.

This dissertation introduces, formally develops, and theoretically validates the Autonomous Habitat Isolation Gateway (AHIG): a distributed AI-driven cyber-physical safety architecture in which every pressure gateway — every door, hatch, and airlock — functions as an intelligent autonomous safety node embedded in a colony-wide neural network. Rather than serving as passive barriers, AHIG gateways continuously monitor environmental parameters, execute local physics-informed predictive hazard detection, coordinate through distributed consensus protocols, and autonomously reconfigure habitat graph topology to maximize crew survival under catastrophic scenarios.

The dissertation makes seven primary original contributions: (i) a formal graph-theoretic model of habitat topology under dynamic failure; (ii) a Bayesian-LSTM hybrid framework for predictive hazard scoring with physics-informed regularization; (iii) a Markov Decision Process formulation for autonomous gateway action selection with provable performance bounds; (iv) a survival bubble formation algorithm that is optimal for tree-like topologies and heuristically effective in general; (v) a fast Byzantine fault-tolerant consensus protocol with timing guarantees; (vi) a compositional safety verification framework based on probabilistic model checking and rely-guarantee reasoning; and (vii) a human-automation trust taxonomy and a temporal authority model for life-critical extraterrestrial settings.

Collectively, these contributions demonstrate that AHIG is theoretically sound, computationally tractable, and operationally superior to any existing passive habitat containment paradigm. In the proposed architecture, the doors of an extraterrestrial settlement become the neurons of a survival intelligence.

Keywords: Autonomous Safety Systems; Extraterrestrial Habitat Architecture; Distributed Cyber-Physical Systems; Predictive Hazard Detection; Graph Theory; Markov Decision Processes; Formal Verification; Digital Twin; Human-Autonomy Teaming.

