CHAPTER 9: DISTRIBUTED CONSENSUS

AHIG must coordinate gateway actions in a Byzantine environment where some nodes may fail or be compromised.

AHIG Fast Consensus Protocol (AFCP). We design a leader‑based Byzantine fault‑tolerant protocol, inspired by PBFT but tuned for low latency. The network consists of $N$ gateway nodes, of which at most $f < N/3$ may be faulty. A distinguished leader (rotating view) proposes a state change (e.g., SEAL bubble perimeter). The protocol proceeds in three phases:

1. Pre‑prepare: The leader sends a signed proposal message to all nodes.
2. Prepare: Upon receiving a valid proposal, each correct node multicasts a prepare vote. A node waits for $2f+1$ matching prepare messages (including its own) to certify the proposal order.
3. Commit: After the prepare certificate, nodes multicast commit votes. Once $2f+1$ commits are collected, the action is executed and an acknowledgment is sent.

A view change is triggered if the leader fails to make progress within $\Delta_{\text{timeout}} = 3\ell_{\max} + \delta_{\text{compute}}$, where $\ell_{\max}$ is the maximum message latency and $\delta_{\text{compute}}$ the processing time.

Theorem 9.1 (Agreement and Liveness). Under the partial synchrony assumption (eventually bounded message delay), AFCP ensures that all correct gateways agree on the same sequence of actions, and every valid client request eventually commits.

Proof. The protocol is a standard BFT state machine replication construction; safety follows from the quorum intersection property (any two quorums of size $2f+1$ intersect in at least one correct node), preventing conflicting commits. Liveness is guaranteed by the view‑change mechanism when a new correct leader is elected. $\square$

Time Bound. In the steady state, a consensus instance completes in at most $3\ell_{\max} + \delta_{\text{compute}}$ (pre‑prepare → prepare → commit). With $\ell_{\max} \le 5$ ms (HIN latency) and $\delta_{\text{compute}} \le 1$ ms, the consensus latency is $< 20$ ms, satisfying the real‑time requirement.

Local Safety Fallback. If a node loses connectivity and cannot participate in consensus for a duration exceeding a threshold, it defaults to the sealed state (CLOSE) and follows its local BLHP policy, ensuring conservative isolation until network recovery.

