Appendix C: Bayesian‑LSTM Hybrid Predictor – Detailed Physics Loss

C.1 Inter‑Module Flow Equation

We derive the mass flow rate between two modules $i$ and $j$ separated by a gateway with openness $\sigma \in [0,1]$. The flow is assumed compressible through an orifice of effective area $A_{\text{eff}}(\sigma) = \sigma A_{\max}$. When the pressure ratio $P_j / P_i$ is below the critical ratio $r_c = (2/(\gamma+1))^{\gamma/(\gamma-1)}$, the flow is choked; otherwise it is subsonic. For a diatomic gas ($\gamma=1.4$), $r_c \approx 0.528$. The mass flow rate from $i$ to $j$ is:

\dot{m}_{ij} = 
\begin{cases}
C_d A_{\text{eff}} P_i \sqrt{\frac{\gamma}{R T_i}} \left(\frac{2}{\gamma+1}\right)^{\frac{\gamma+1}{2(\gamma-1)}}, & \frac{P_j}{P_i} \le r_c \;\; \text{(choked)}\\
C_d A_{\text{eff}} P_i \sqrt{\frac{2\gamma}{(\gamma-1)R T_i} \left[ \left(\frac{P_j}{P_i}\right)^{2/\gamma} - \left(\frac{P_j}{P_i}\right)^{(\gamma+1)/\gamma} \right]}, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}


where $C_d$ is the discharge coefficient ($\approx 0.62$). The sign is positive when $P_i > P_j$, otherwise the flow reverses.

C.2 Pressure Evolution Physics Loss

The pressure change in module $i$ due to gas exchange is obtained from mass conservation and the ideal gas law $P_i V_i = m_i R T_i$ (assuming isothermal conditions for simplicity, or we can include temperature changes). The mass balance:
\frac{d m_i}{dt} = - \sum_{j \in N(i)} \dot{m}_{ij} \;\; \Rightarrow \;\; \frac{d P_i}{dt} = - \frac{R T_i}{V_i} \sum_{j \in N(i)} \dot{m}_{ij}.

The physics‑informed regularisation term penalises the discrepancy between the LSTM‑predicted pressure $\hat{P}_i(t+\Delta t)$ and the pressure obtained by integrating this ODE for one time step:
\mathcal{L}_{\text{press}} = \sum_{(i,j) \in E} \left( \hat{P}_i(t+\Delta t) - P_i(t) + \frac{\Delta t \, R T_i}{V_i} \dot{m}_{ij}(t) \right)^2,


where the sum runs over all ordered pairs of connected modules (flow contributions are accounted appropriately). This ensures that any predicted pressure change respects the physical gas flow through the gateways.

Similarly, we add terms for thermal energy conservation and species mass balances for O₂ and CO₂. The full regularisation term is:
\mathcal{L}_{\text{physics}} = \lambda_p \mathcal{L}_{\text{press}} + \lambda_T \mathcal{L}_{\text{thermal}} + \lambda_s \mathcal{L}_{\text{species}}.

