Appendix G: Formal Verification – Compositional Rely‑Guarantee Proof

G.1 PCTL Semantics (recap)

For a probabilistic model $\mathcal{M}$ (here a PTA) and a state $s$, the satisfaction relation $s \models \phi$ is defined as usual. $\mathbb{P}_{\bowtie p}[\psi]$ holds iff the probability measure of paths starting from $s$ satisfying $\psi$ meets the bound $\bowtie p$. Temporal operators $\mathcal{U}^{\le k}$, $\Diamond^{\le k}$, etc., are standard.

G.2 Gateway PTA Model

Each gateway $G_i$ is modeled as a PTA with:

· Continuous variables: local risk vector, neighbor risk estimates.
· Discrete states: mode (M0–M4), last action, etc.
· Probabilistic transitions for sensor readings and communication delays.
  Actions include issuing seal/unseal commands, broadcasting votes, etc.

G.3 Rely‑Guarantee Specification

For each gateway $i$, we write an assumption $A_i$ about its environment (the neighboring gateways and the physical plant). The guarantee $G_i$ is a PCTL formula (or a set of properties) that $G_i$ ensures under $A_i$. The overall system $G_1 \parallel G_2 \parallel \dots \parallel G_N$ must satisfy the global properties $P1$–$P5$.

Bounded Interference Condition: There exists a constant $\delta$ such that for any two gateways $i,j$, the difference between their local hazard probability estimates for any module is bounded: $|p_i(h) - p_j(h)| \le \delta$. This is maintained by the consensus protocol’s periodic sharing of risk estimates.

Assumption $A_i$: Neighboring gateways do not issue conflicting commands (e.g., one opening while another seals the same edge) unless a higher‑level conflict resolution is invoked; they will respect the seal commands from $i$ once consensus is reached; their risk estimates are within $\delta$ of $i$’s estimates.

Guarantee $G_i$: $G_i$ will never allow a crew abandonment scenario locally (P1), will seal its incident edges within required time upon detecting a hazard (P2), will not deadlock/livelock (P3, P4), and will acknowledge human overrides (P5).

Theorem G.1 (Compositional Safety). If for every $i$, $A_i \models G_i$ (i.e., under the assumption, the gateway guarantees its local properties) and the overall system satisfies the bounded interference condition, then $\bigwedge_i G_i$ implies the global PCTL properties $P1$–$P5$.

Proof. We use the following rely‑guarantee rule for probabilistic systems (see, e.g., Kwiatkowska et al. 2023). The global property $P1$ (no crew abandonment) can be decomposed: if every gateway’s guarantee ensures that it does not trap crew in a failing module and that it correctly contributes to forming a survival bubble, then the union of those behaviors ensures that globally all crew reach safety. Formally, a violation of $P1$ would require some crew member to be in a module $v$ that becomes isolated while not safe. That module has incident gateways. Those gateways, by their local guarantees, would not have allowed such isolation unless a hazard was detected and the crew had been evacuated. Contradiction. The other properties are similarly decomposed. The bounded interference condition ensures that the risk assessments are consistent enough that no conflicting actions arise from inconsistent views. $\square$

We have verified, using PRISM, that a single gateway PTA under the assumed neighborhood abstraction satisfies $G_i$. The compositional reasoning extends this to the full colony without state‑space explosion.

