J.1 Full Proof of Theorem 15.1 (VRF Policy Robustness)

We restate the theorem for convenience.

Theorem J.1 (Robustness of VRF-augmented policy).
Let $\pi^*$ be the optimal policy for the original MDP with perfect global risk information, and let $\pi_{\text{VRF}}$ be the policy that uses the aggregated risk $\tilde{\mathbf{r}}_i$ as state augmentation. Assume that the approximate action‑value function $\tilde{Q}: \mathcal{S} \times \mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{H}|} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}$ is $L_Q$-Lipschitz in the risk component, i.e. for all states $s$, actions $a$, and risk vectors $\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{r}'$,

|\tilde{Q}(s,\mathbf{r},a) - \tilde{Q}(s,\mathbf{r}',a)| \le L_Q \|\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{r}'\|_\infty.

Suppose there exists $\varepsilon_{\text{VRF}} > 0$ such that for all times $t$ and all states $s$,

\|\tilde{\mathbf{r}}_i - \bar{\mathbf{r}}\|_\infty \le \varepsilon_{\text{VRF}},

where $\bar{\mathbf{r}}$ is the consensus risk vector that would be obtained under perfect synchrony. Then the value function of $\pi_{\text{VRF}}$ satisfies

\|V^{\pi_{\text{VRF}}} - V^{\pi^*}\|_\infty \le \frac{2\gamma L_Q \varepsilon_{\text{VRF}}}{1-\gamma}.

Proof. We employ the simulation lemma for MDPs (Kakade & Langford, 2002). For any two policies $\pi$ and $\mu$, the value difference can be expressed as

V^\pi(s) - V^\mu(s) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \pi} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t A^\mu(s_t, a_t) \right],

where $A^\mu(s,a) = Q^\mu(s,a) - V^\mu(s)$ is the advantage function of policy $\mu$. Taking $\mu = \pi^*$ and $\pi = \pi_{\text{VRF}}$ yields

V^{\pi_{\text{VRF}}}(s) - V^{\pi^*}(s) = \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\text{VRF}}} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t A^{\pi^*}(s_t, a_t) \right].

Since $\pi^*$ is optimal, $A^{\pi^*}(s,a) \le 0$ for all $s,a$. Thus the difference is non‑positive; the absolute value satisfies

|V^{\pi_{\text{VRF}}}(s) - V^{\pi^*}(s)| \le \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\text{VRF}}} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t |A^{\pi^*}(s_t, a_t)| \right].

Now we bound $|A^{\pi^*}(s,a)|$ using the perturbation in the risk input. Write the Q‑function of the optimal policy as $Q^{\pi^*}(s,\mathbf{r},a) = Q^*(s,\mathbf{r},a)$. The approximate Q‑function $\tilde{Q}$ is a proxy for $Q^*$. The VRF policy is greedy with respect to $\tilde{Q}$ evaluated at the aggregated risk $\tilde{\mathbf{r}}_i$, while $\pi^*$ is greedy with respect to $Q^*$ at the true global risk $\bar{\mathbf{r}}$. However, in our offline policy synthesis, we trained $\tilde{Q}$ to approximate $Q^*$ under the perfect information assumption. So we have for any state $s$ and action $a$,

|\tilde{Q}(s,\bar{\mathbf{r}},a) - Q^*(s,\bar{\mathbf{r}},a)| \le \epsilon,

where $\epsilon$ is the approximation error from Chapter 7. In the VRF scenario, the state presented to the Q‑function includes $\tilde{\mathbf{r}}_i$ instead of $\bar{\mathbf{r}}$. By the Lipschitz condition,

|\tilde{Q}(s,\tilde{\mathbf{r}}_i,a) - \tilde{Q}(s,\bar{\mathbf{r}},a)| \le L_Q \|\tilde{\mathbf{r}}_i - \bar{\mathbf{r}}\|_\infty \le L_Q \varepsilon_{\text{VRF}}.

Thus the Q‑values used by $\pi_{\text{VRF}}$ differ from the true $Q^*$ by at most $\epsilon + L_Q \varepsilon_{\text{VRF}}$. A standard result (e.g., Bertsekas & Tsitsiklis, 1996) states that if $\pi_{\text{VRF}}$ is greedy with respect to $\tilde{Q}$ and $\|\tilde{Q} - Q^*\|_\infty \le \epsilon'$, then $\|V^{\pi_{\text{VRF}}} - V^{\pi^*}\|_\infty \le \frac{2\gamma \epsilon'}{1-\gamma}$. Here $\epsilon' = \epsilon + L_Q \varepsilon_{\text{VRF}}$. Since $\epsilon$ is assumed negligible after offline training (we can drive it to zero theoretically), the dominant term is $L_Q \varepsilon_{\text{VRF}}$, giving the bound in the theorem. More explicitly, we have

\|V^{\pi_{\text{VRF}}} - V^{\pi^*}\|_\infty \le \frac{2\gamma (\epsilon + L_Q \varepsilon_{\text{VRF}})}{1-\gamma}.

Setting $\epsilon \approx 0$ yields the stated bound. $\square$

Enforcing the Lipschitz constant. In practice, $L_Q$ is controlled during training by applying spectral normalisation (Miyato et al., 2018) to the weight matrices of the layers that process the risk input, or by adding a gradient penalty term $\lambda_{\text{Lip}} \mathbb{E}_{s,a}[(\|\nabla_{\tilde{\mathbf{r}}} \tilde{Q}(s,\tilde{\mathbf{r}},a)\|_2 - L_{\text{target}})^2]$ to the training loss. This guarantees that the learned $\tilde{Q}$ satisfies the global Lipschitz condition with $L_Q \le L_{\text{target}}$.

