J.3 Detailed Compositional Proof of Theorem 15.3 (VRF‑Extended Rely‑Guarantee)

J.3.1 Formal Definitions

We model each gateway $G_i$ as a Probabilistic Timed Automaton (PTA) with state space $S_i$, actions $\text{Act}_i$, and a transition relation incorporating both discrete and continuous dynamics. The full AHIG system is the parallel composition $\mathcal{M} = G_1 \parallel G_2 \parallel \dots \parallel G_N$, with synchronisation on shared actions (gateway state changes) and message exchanges.

For each gateway we define two operational modes:

· Consensus mode (mode $C$): $\delta_{\text{eff}}(t) \ge \delta_{\text{critical}}$; the gateway participates in AFCP and maintains the original bounded interference assumption $A_i^{\text{cons}}$.
· VRF mode (mode $V$): $\delta_{\text{eff}}(t) < \delta_{\text{critical}}$; the gateway switches to the VRF protocol and operates under assumption $A_i^{\text{VRF}}$.

The assumptions and guarantees are formulated in PCTL over the local state variables. Let $\mathbf{r}_i$ be the local risk vector, and $\mathbf{r}_j^{(i)}$ the last received risk from neighbor $j$.

Assumption $A_i^{\text{cons}}$ (original):
\mathbb{P}_{=1}\left[ \max_{j \in N(i)} \|\mathbf{r}_i - \mathbf{r}_j^{(i)}\|_\infty \le \delta_{\text{base}} \right].

Assumption $A_i^{\text{VRF}}$ (relaxed):
\mathbb{P}_{> 1 - \epsilon_{\text{net}}}\left[ \max_{j \in N(i)} \|\mathbf{r}_i - \mathbf{r}_j^{(i)}\|_\infty \le \delta_{\max} \right],


with $\delta_{\max} > \delta_{\text{base}}$ and a small network failure probability $\epsilon_{\text{net}}$.

Guarantee $G_i^{\text{cons}}$ (original): States that under $A_i^{\text{cons}}$, the gateway satisfies local projections of P1–P5 with original probability bounds (e.g., P2 with $\mathbb{P}_{\ge 0.9999}$).

Guarantee $G_i^{\text{VRF}}$ (relaxed): Under $A_i^{\text{VRF}}$, the gateway satisfies the same properties with slightly relaxed bounds (e.g., P2 with $\mathbb{P}_{\ge 0.9995}$), due to the additional worst‑case delay introduced by the information discount.

J.3.2 Mode‑Switch Logic

The system‑wide mode is determined by the minimum of $\delta_{\text{eff}}$ across all gateways. To avoid chattering, a hysteresis is implemented:

· Enter VRF when $\min_i \delta_{\text{eff},i}(t) < \delta_{\text{critical}} - \Delta_{\text{hyst}}$ for a continuous interval of length $T_{\text{confirm}}$.
· Return to consensus when $\min_i \delta_{\text{eff},i}(t) > \delta_{\text{critical}} + \Delta_{\text{hyst}}$ for $T_{\text{confirm}}$.

This ensures that the system does not oscillate between modes within a single hazard event.

J.3.3 Proof of Global Safety Properties

Theorem J.3 (Compositional safety under VRF). Under the mode‑switch logic, and assuming that each gateway satisfies its local guarantees when the corresponding assumption holds, the global system $\mathcal{M}$ satisfies the PCTL properties P1–P5 with the following adjusted bounds:

· P1: $\mathbb{P}_{\ge 0.998}$
· P2: $\mathbb{P}_{\ge 0.9995}$
· P3: $\mathbb{P}_{=1}$
· P4: $\mathbb{P}_{=1}$
· P5: $\mathbb{P}_{\ge 0.9995}$

Proof. We partition the set of execution traces into those where all gateways remain in consensus mode for the entire hazard‑response interval, and those where at least one gateway enters VRF mode during the critical window.

Case 1: Pure consensus traces. For these traces, the original assumptions $A_i^{\text{cons}}$ hold throughout, and the original guarantees $G_i^{\text{cons}}$ apply. By the original composition proof (Chapter 10), properties P1–P5 are satisfied with the original tight bounds. The probability measure of such traces is at least $(1 - p_{\text{VRF}})$, where $p_{\text{VRF}}$ is the probability that the system enters VRF during a hazard. Even if we ignore this case’s high probability, the worst‑case bounds will be dominated by the VRF traces.

Case 2: Traces containing VRF mode. For any trace where some gateway is in VRF mode during the response, the system‑wide assumption $A^{\text{VRF}} = \bigwedge_i A_i^{\text{VRF}}$ holds with probability at least $1 - N\epsilon_{\text{net}}$, by the union bound and the definition of $A_i^{\text{VRF}}$. Under $A^{\text{VRF}}$, every gateway satisfies its relaxed guarantee $G_i^{\text{VRF}}$. We now argue compositionally that the conjunction of local guarantees implies the global properties.

Consider property P2 (hazard containment). For a hazard $h$ in module $v_k$, all incident gateways must seal. Each incident gateway $i$ has a local property $G_i^{\text{VRF}}$ stating that, under its local assumption, it will seal within a specified time with probability $\ge 0.9995$. Because the sealing of different gateways involves independent physical actuation (conditionally independent given the event), the probability that all incident gateways seal is at least the product of their individual probabilities (a conservative lower bound). If the number of incident gateways is at most 4, the joint probability is $\ge 0.9995^4 \approx 0.998$. For larger numbers, a more refined analysis using the fact that a single failure of one gateway to seal would still leave others functioning (the bubble may still be formed) yields even higher survival probability. For simplicity, we take the conservative product bound, yielding the advertised $\mathbb{P}_{\ge 0.998}$. Similar reasoning applies to P1 and P5. Properties P3 and P4 are deterministic in the absence of deadlocks/livelocks, which remain absent by the local guarantees.

The overall probability of a violation of any safety property is bounded by the sum of the probability that $A^{\text{VRF}}$ fails (at most $N\epsilon_{\text{net}}$) plus the probability that, even under $A^{\text{VRF}}$, some local guarantee is violated. By choosing $\epsilon_{\text{net}}$ sufficiently small (e.g., $10^{-6}$) and calibrating the relaxed guarantees, the total violation probability remains below the required thresholds (e.g., $0.002$ for P1). $\square$

J.3.4 Numerical Calibration

For a settlement with $N = 50$ gateways, setting $\epsilon_{\text{net}} = 10^{-6}$ yields a total assumption failure probability of $5 \times 10^{-5}$, negligible compared to the $10^{-3}$ tolerance in P1. The relaxed bounds (e.g., $0.9995$ instead of $0.9999$) account for the additional VRF delays and are verified via probabilistic model checking of a single gateway PTA with the VRF state augmentation.

