J.4 Derivation of Path Viability Probability

The path viability indicator $\mathbf{1}_{\text{path exists}}(a)$ in Chapter 15 is generalized to a probability $\mathcal{P}_{\text{path}}(a, \Delta t_{\text{isolation}})$ that decays with the isolation time. The underlying model assumes that downstream module failures occur as a Poisson process with rate $\lambda_{\text{fail}}$ per module, and that any failure along the path renders it non‑viable. During the isolation period $\Delta t_{\text{isolation}}$, the number of modules on the path that could have failed is $K_{\text{path}}$. Under the worst‑case assumption that failures are independent, the probability that no module on the path has failed is

\mathcal{P}_{\text{path}}(a, \Delta t_{\text{isolation}}) = e^{-\lambda_{\text{fail}} \, K_{\text{path}} \, \Delta t_{\text{isolation}}}.

We set $\lambda_{\text{path}} = \lambda_{\text{fail}} K_{\text{path}}$ to obtain the exponential form used in the chapter. The pre‑compiled map provides $K_{\text{path}}$ for each action, and $\lambda_{\text{fail}}$ is estimated from colony‑wide reliability data (e.g., mean time between failures of critical components). This probability ensures that as $\Delta t_{\text{isolation}}$ grows, the gateway becomes more conservative about opening toward paths whose integrity cannot be verified.

