J.7 Actuator Duty‑Cycle Formalisation

To protect the gateway’s brushless DC motors from excessive wear during rapid hazard fluctuations, we implement a temporal filter in the actuation command path.

Let $t_{\text{last}}$ be the timestamp of the last successful mechanical state change (fully completed), and let $\tau_{\text{motor}} = 2$ s be the minimum inter‑actuation interval derived from the motor’s thermal time constant. The filter operates as a finite state machine:

· If a new command $a_{\text{cmd}}$ is issued while $t - t_{\text{last}} < \tau_{\text{motor}}$, and the command would reverse the direction of the last actuation (i.e., from closing to opening or vice versa), the command is queued and executed only when the cooldown elapses, unless an emergency override flag is set.
· Emergency override: If the TEV algorithm decides that an immediate actuation is required to prevent loss of life (e.g., Pulsed Venting Close protocol), the filter is bypassed and the command is executed instantly, resetting $t_{\text{last}}$. After an emergency override, the filter is suspended for a short period to allow multiple rapid moves if needed, then re‑engaged.

Formally, let $E \in \{0,1\}$ be the emergency flag (0 = normal, 1 = life‑critical escape). The effective action $a_{\text{eff}}$ executed at time $t$ is:

a_{\text{eff}} = 
\begin{cases}
a_{\text{cmd}} & \text{if } E = 1 \;\text{or}\; (t - t_{\text{last}} \ge \tau_{\text{motor}}) \;\text{or}\; \text{sign}(\Delta a) = \text{sign}(\text{last move}),\\
\text{hold current state} & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}

This logic is implemented in the gateway actuator controller, not in the AI policy, ensuring that the theoretical optimality of the MDP decisions is unaffected (the policy is not aware of the filter; it simply issues commands, and the filter manages hardware protection).



